                <pb facs="0084" n="74" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv10/pg74"/>

                <head>Appendix 32</head>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Recommendation</hi> [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">From the Food Section</hi>] <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Supreme Economic Council Regarding Preliminaries of Peace</hi></p>
                <p>The Food Section adopted the following resolution:— <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Food Section do not desire to recommend the imposition by the
                            Preliminary Peace Terms of any limitation of food into
                        Germany.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>In making the above recommendation, the Food Section call the attention of the
                    Supreme Economic Council to the fact that, while the world supplies are in
                    general sufficient to cover the world needs, this is mainly a question of
                    shipping, and in giving out their opinion they desire to make it clear that they
                    are basing their judgment on the assumption that the world shipping and finance
                    will be so regulated as to make it possible to secure distribution of the world
                    supplies.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">March</hi> 15, 1919.</p>
            </div>
